// The one allocation the crypto modules make for key and big-number memory, out of line and in a section of its
// own. A release build inlines the allocator (its size checks, free lists and slow-path calls) into whatever
// allocates, and those branch on the heap's state, which tools/ct/check-asm.py can't tell from a secret. Here the
// allocation is a call to a function in the section `.text.abla_ct_allocate`, which the checker doesn't read: it requires the size to be public and
// takes the result as a pointer to an object whose header is public (tools/test-ct.sh, header_allocator). Nothing
// else calls it; inlining it would remove that call, so the test fails if no release object calls a function of that section. (An
// `@export` would give the symbol a stable name, but it also pulls the export machinery into every program that
// imports a crypto module: about 800 bytes.)

import "abla/unsafe/memory"
import "abla/unsafe/memory/raw"

@noinline @section(".text.abla_ct_allocate")
trusted fun cryptoAllocate(size: int): int =
    ablaUnsafePointerAddress(ablaUnsafeAllocate(size))
